course: everyday
lesson: 347
title: Leaving someone out
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Kai in Perth teased a boy because his clothes were old, to make the others laugh. The boy stopped coming out at playtime, and his mum told the teacher. Kai said sorry, and the next time someone teased the boy he stood up for him. Money and clothes don't decide who someone is.
explain: Kai felt good about changing — and a few weeks later slipped. His friend got a wrong answer in maths and Kai laughed out loud. She stopped putting her hand up. When the teacher asked why she was so quiet, Kai's face went hot. He said sorry and cheered her right answers from then on. Everyone slips; what matters is putting it right each time.
explain: Some words are worse than they sound. A girl told a classmate she couldn't be the princess because of her skin colour — something she had heard somewhere and copied. Her classmate cried; the head teacher and her parents talked with her about why it was wrong. She said sorry, and in the next game her classmate was the princess and she was the guard. Anyone can be anything in a game.
explain: Telling everyone a friend's secret (that he still sleeps with a teddy) got the whole class teasing him, and he stopped trusting Kai. Kai said sorry and told the class that lots of people have teddies — even him.
fact: Teasing someone about old clothes or having less money is unfair; it says nothing about who they are.
fact: Saying someone can't be or do something because of their skin colour is wrong and hurtful.
fact: Saying a girl can't play football because she is a girl is wrong; girls play football at every level.
fact: Laughing at someone's wrong answer stops them trying.
fact: Words about someone's body can hurt deeply and for a long time. A boy called «fat» in the changing room began hiding when he changed, and the teacher talked to the parents; the boy who said it put it right by saying sorry and saying the true thing — that the other boy is a great runner.
fact: Hiding someone's bag «as a joke» can make them miss the bus and worry their family.
fact: Telling a friend's private secret to others breaks trust.
fact: Sharing with the person next to you is a small kindness that makes everyone's work better. A girl who wouldn't share her crayons felt mean when her neighbour's grey picture went up on the wall.
fact: After you stop teasing, you may slip again; the fix is to say sorry and put it right each time.
fact: Standing up for someone who is teased is a strong way to make amends.
exercise: Why did Kai tease the boy about his old clothes? | To make the others laugh and look big | Because the clothes were dirty and smelled | Because the boy asked him to say it aloud | To help the boy get some new clothes soon
exercise: How did Kai put the clothes teasing right? | Said sorry and stood up for the boy next time | Gave the boy his old clothes and said nothing more | Told the teacher he was joking | Avoided the boy
exercise: Weeks later Kai laughed at a friend's wrong answer. What does this show? | People slip back, and each slip must be put right | Laughing at answers is different from teasing, so it doesn't count | Kai was never really sorry | Once you change, you can't slip
exercise: What happened when Kai laughed at his friend's wrong answer? | She stopped putting her hand up | She tried harder at once to show them | She laughed along with all of them | Nothing happened, she didn't mind
exercise: A girl says a classmate can't be the princess because of her skin colour. Why is it wrong? | Skin colour doesn't decide what anyone can be | It's fine if it's only a game in the playground | Princesses must all look the same | It's only wrong if a teacher hears
exercise: That girl had copied the idea from something she heard. Does that make it all right? | No, she still needed to learn why it was wrong and say sorry | Yes, it wasn't her idea, she only did what the bigger girls told her | Yes, if she's young | Yes, if she didn't mean it
exercise: A boy hides another boy's bag as a joke. The boy misses the bus. What makes it not a joke? | The boy wasn't laughing, and it caused real trouble | The bag was heavy, so it was a favour to hide it | The bus was late anyway | Jokes never cause trouble
exercise: A boy says a girl can't play football because she's a girl. She scores the next day. What should he do? | Say sorry and pass her the ball | Say it was only luck that she scored | Leave the game and go off in a sulk | Say girls can only ever play in goal
exercise: A boy calls another «fat» in the changing room after losing a race to him. What happens? | The boy hides when changing, and the teacher talks to the parents | The boy laughs and forgets it, because names on the field don't really stick | Nothing, it's just a word | The boy runs slower next time
exercise: How did that boy put the «fat» remark right? | Said sorry and said the other boy is a great runner | Called him something nicer behind his back instead | Said he was only joking | Said nothing and stayed away
exercise: Why did Kai's friend stop trusting him after the teddy secret? | Kai told the whole class something private | Kai took his teddy into school to show everyone | Kai sleeps with a teddy too | Kai laughed at his drawing
exercise: How did Kai help undo the teddy teasing? | Said sorry and told the class lots of people have teddies, even him | Bought his friend a new teddy with his own pocket money and said nothing to the class | Said the friend made it up | Stopped talking to everyone
exercise: A girl won't share crayons and her neighbour's picture is grey. When does she feel mean? | When the grey picture goes on the wall | Never, the crayons are hers to keep | When her own picture fades | When the teacher buys more
exercise: What is a strong way to make amends after teasing someone? | Stand up for them when others tease | Give them sweets once to say you're sorry | Avoid them forever | Tell them to ignore teasing
exercise: Which is the wise choice when your group starts laughing at someone? | Don't join in, and say it's not funny | Laugh quietly so you still fit in with them | Walk off and say nothing ever | Laugh but not too long
test: «his trainers are well old lol, does his mum buy em at a jumble sale». What's the kind reply? | «Leave it, it's nothing to do with who he is» | «Ha, yeah, and his shoes are older than my dad» | «Mine are newer» | «Say it louder»
test: Kai: «I said sorry for teasing last month, so laughing at her answer doesn't count». What's true? | It's a new slip and still needs a sorry | It doesn't count after one sorry, the first covers it | Laughing at answers is fine | She should toughen up
test: Head teacher, calm: «Why did you say she couldn't be the princess?» What's the right way forward? | Understand it was wrong, say sorry and play fairly | Say it was only a game, princesses aren't real anyway | Say everyone says it | Say nothing
test: Mum on the phone: «Our son missed the bus because someone hid his bag.» The boy who hid it was joking. What should he do? | Own up and say sorry | Say the bag hid itself | Say it was really funny | Blame his mates for it
test: Lads at football: «no girls, she'll be rubbish». She's the best scorer in Year 5. What's true? | Girls can play as well as anyone, so let her in | Girls can't play football | She can only watch | Only if she's in goal
test: Teacher: «Someone said something about Owen's body in the changing room. He won't change now.» What is right for the boy who said it? | Own up, say sorry and say something true and kind | Say it was only a joke and nobody else seemed to mind | Say Owen is too sensitive | Say nothing
test: «guess what, Sam still has a teddy!!» – the whole class laughs. What does that do? | Breaks Sam's trust and gets him teased | Makes Sam popular | Nothing, everyone knows | Helps Sam grow up
test: Kid: «I laughed at her wrong answer and she never noticed, so no harm». What's true? | She might have; laughing at mistakes stops people trying | No harm done if she didn't look round, she never knew | Wrong answers are funny | It's only harmful in tests
test: Girl next to her: «can I use your red?» What is the kind answer? | «Sure, here» | «No, get your own» | «Only if you pay» | «After I'm done with all of them»
test: Which is TRUE about leaving someone out because they're different? | It hurts them and says nothing true about them | It keeps groups strong, everyone knows who's in | It's fine if they are new | It's only wrong if a teacher sees
test: «just say sorry, then you can do it again». Why is that wrong? | A real sorry means trying not to do it again | Sorry works every time | Sorries don't matter | Only grown-ups say sorry
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the exclusion of a child from a role in play on account of skin colour. | It is wrong and hurtful, skin colour determining nothing of what a person may be | It is permissible within imaginative play, since the roles assigned are not real ones | It is wrong only where a teacher is present | It is of no consequence to the child excluded
exam: Identify the principal effect upon a pupil of laughter directed at his incorrect answers. | He becomes less willing to attempt answers | He becomes more confident | He ceases to make errors | It has no effect whatsoever
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the disclosure to a class of a private matter confided by a friend? | It constitutes a breach of trust which exposes the friend to ridicule | It strengthens the friendship, since the matter is now openly known to all | It constitutes a harmless amusement | It is permissible where the matter is trivial
exam: Determine the proper response of a child who, having ceased to tease others, subsequently repeats such conduct. | He ought to apologise once more and make amends on this occasion also | He ought to regard the earlier apology as sufficient | He ought to abandon all effort to improve | He ought to conceal the repetition
